  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple schematic of DNA replication encountering a secondary DNA structure. A replication fork is shown moving to the right, with the replisome positioned at the fork junction. The parental DNA separates into two strands, labeled as lagging strands above and below, while a dashed line appears to represent newly synthesized DNA on one side.\n\nAhead of the replisome on the right, the DNA contains a small looped structure labeled “intramolecular G4,” indicating a G-quadruplex formed within a single DNA strand. The placement of this structure directly in front of the advancing fork suggests that it acts as an obstacle during replication. The overall message is that as the replication machinery progresses along DNA, it can encounter stable noncanonical structures such as G4s that may impede fork movement or require special processing.\n\nVisually, the emphasis is on the spatial relationship between fork progression, the replisome, and the folded DNA structure rather than on molecular detail. The diagram therefore illustrates a replication challenge caused by an intramolecular G-quadruplex on the template ahead of the replication fork.",
